---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/guides/selection-framework';
const title = "Choose a Shopify email tool with a defensible decision record";
const desc = "Compare finalists against your actual workflows, operating capacity and commercial constraints.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "How many finalists should I test?",
    "answer": "Use a manageable set that covers the required buying jobs. There is no universal three-vendor limit; each added dependency needs an owner."
  },
  {
    "question": "When is a simpler sender enough?",
    "answer": "When it demonstrates the required workflows, data, controls and operator fit within the commercial constraints. Order count alone does not decide."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can the cheapest quote win?",
    "answer": "Yes, among candidates that meet mandatory requirements. Include implementation, overlap, renewal and exit costs before comparing."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should block a decision?",
    "answer": "An unresolved mandatory gate, unavailable evidence or an unowned workaround. Keep these explicit rather than averaging them away."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">All operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16"><article class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Write the buying brief</h2>
    <p>List the workflows that must work, the present failure, affected customers, required channels and available operator time. Separate mandatory capabilities from preferences. Identify the person who will maintain rules after launch and who approves data, consent and cost decisions. A familiar logo or a template gallery is not evidence that the required workflow works.</p>
    <h2>Use pass/fail gates before weighted preferences</h2>
    <p>A missing mandatory exclusion cannot be offset by a nicer editor. For each gate record the precise requirement, demonstration, evidence location, owner and unresolved issue. Only compare preferences among survivors. If using weighted scores, agree weights before demos and define what each score means; do not assign points from this site’s rankings or universal order-count thresholds.</p>
    <h2>Six dimensions with concrete evidence</h2>
    <p>Lifecycle fit: build the required branches and exclusions. Data: trace each needed field from source to sender and observe updates. Economics: obtain dated quotes for ordinary, peak and growth usage. Capacity: have the future owner make and reverse a change. Channels: demonstrate consent and priority across the proposed stack. Exit risk: export identities, preferences, rules and reporting needed by the successor. Preserve workarounds as costs rather than marking them native passes.</p>
    <h2>Proposed pen-store demonstration</h2>
    <p>For a proposed high-consideration pen store, ask finalists to send compatible-stock content to an explicit nib preference with a neutral fallback. Include a gift buyer, returned item, unknown preference and a sold-out variant. Require evidence for each branch and inspect the preview before any controlled send. The point is product relevance and operator control, not an assumed vendor winner.</p>
    <h2>Proposed tea-store demonstration</h2>
    <p>For a proposed tea store, build a family-specific reminder that excludes active supply subscriptions. Change a subscription to paused and then unknown, add a new family order and replay a delayed event. Record membership and queued-message behavior. Ask the operator to change the family interval and explain the consequences. This exposes data freshness and maintainability that a generic cart demo misses.</p>
    <h2>Keep a useful candidate catalog</h2>
    <p>Use the app directory to discover candidates, then request evidence for the buying job: primary email automation, SMS, onsite capture, transactional updates or loyalty. A specialist may complement the primary sender; count duplicate capture and overlapping dispatch responsibilities. Sequenzy is a candidate to evaluate under the same gates, not a default winner. Directory and comparison links are research starting points, not current product certification.</p>
    <h2>Commercial comparison and sensitivity</h2>
    <p>Give finalists the same workload definitions, including billable contacts, sends, seats, SMS destinations, onboarding and overlapping migration months. Ask what happens when usage exceeds a tier and what stops at the cap. Keep a dated quote and contract exclusions. Model renewal and exit as well as the first invoice; do not assume inactive records are free or that creating an extra segment creates an extra billable customer.</p>
    <h2>Pilot acceptance differs from revenue proof</h2>
    <p>Use a test store or carefully controlled audience to verify event integrity, suppression, content fallback, edit ownership and export completeness. These are proposed acceptance tests, not tests performed for this guide. Set the schedule by access, dependencies and purchase cycles rather than a fixed two-week promise. A small technical pilot cannot establish long-run incremental profit; plan measurement separately.</p>
    <h2>Record the decision and revisit it</h2>
    <p>Keep required gates, evidence dates, known gaps, workaround owners, accepted costs and rejected alternatives. State why the selected configuration is preferable under today’s constraints. Reopen the decision when a required channel, catalog model, staffing arrangement or contract changes. If no candidate passes, resolve the data or workflow gap before treating a total score as permission to migrate.</p>
    <h2>Data evidence and next steps</h2>
    <p>Shopify’s <a class="link" href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/admin-graphql/latest/objects/Order">Order reference</a> documents purchase and refund data and the default restriction on older order access. The <a class="link" href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/admin-graphql/latest/objects/Customer">Customer reference</a> describes history and marketing preferences. Checked November 25, 2026; neither establishes what a particular email connector imports. Confirm history coverage before treating missing records as no purchases.</p>
    <p>Continue with <a class="link" href="/guides/revenue-attribution">revenue measurement</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/segmentation">audience rules</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">tool selection</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">cost modeling</a>, or the <a class="link" href="/apps">app directory</a>.</p>
  </article></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
